'''''Sitobolium punctilobulum''''', the '''eastern hayscented fern'''<ref>{{PLANTS|id=DEPU2|taxon=Dennstaedtia punctilobula|accessdate=13 August 2016}}</ref> or '''hay-scented fern''', is a species of [[fern]] native to eastern [[North America]], from [[Newfoundland (island)|Newfoundland]] west to [[Wisconsin]] and [[Arkansas]], and south in the [[Appalachian Mountains]] to northern [[Alabama]]; it is most abundant in the east of its range, with only scattered populations in the west.<ref name=fna>{{cite encyclopedia | year=1993 | title=Flora of North America | chapter=Dennstaedtia punctilobula |editor=Flora of North America Editorial Committee | last1=Nauman | first1=Clifton E. | last2=Evans | first2=A. Murray |encyclopedia=Flora of North America North of Mexico |volume=2 |publisher=Oxford University Press |location=New York and Oxford |chapter-url=http://www.efloras.org/florataxon.aspx?flora_id=1&taxon_id=200003214}}</ref>

It is a [[deciduous]] fern with [[frond]]s growing to 40–100&nbsp;cm (rarely 130&nbsp;cm) tall and 10–30&nbsp;cm broad; the fronds are bipinnate, with pinnatifid pinnules about three times as long as broad. It occurs in damp or dry acidic soils in woods or open woods, from sea level up to 1,200 m altitude.<ref name=fna/>

''Sitobolium punctilobulum'' can exhibit varying degrees of [[phototropism]]. The [[common name]] "Hay-scented Fern" comes from the fact that crushing it produces an aroma of fresh [[hay]].

The presence of ''Sitobolium punctilobulum'' influences the dynamics of the [[understory]] vegetation of many forests in the eastern United States. An abundance of ''[[Rubus allegheniensis]]'' in open areas encourages new tree seedlings. Where the effects of herbivorous animals (such as [[deer]]) reduce the abundance of ''Rubus allegheniensis'', ''Sitobolium punctilobulum'', which is not browsed by deer, takes over. Where ''Sitobolium punctilobulum'' becomes common, the growth of tree seedlings is restricted.<ref name=PGE>{{cite book | chapter=Wildlife Management | title=The Princeton Guide to Ecology | editor-last=Levin | editor-first=Simon A. | editor-link=Simon A. Levin | location=Princeton |publisher=Princeton University Press | year=2009}}</ref>

It was first described as ''Nephrodium punctilobulum'' by [[André Michaux]] in 1803, and has been known by a variety of [[Synonym (taxonomy)|synonyms]].

